# /issue — Create GitHub Issues with Enforced Conventions

**Usage**: `/issue` or `/issue $ARGUMENTS`

## Current State

- Open issues: !`gh issue list --state open --limit 5 2>/dev/null || echo "unable to fetch"`
- Current branch: !`git branch --show-current`

**Examples**:
```
/issue                                                    # interactive — guided prompts
/issue fix(api): handler returns 500 on empty payload     # quick — title provided, prompts for details
/issue setup-labels                                        # one-time — creates label taxonomy on GitHub
```

---

## What This Command Does

Creates consistently-formatted GitHub Issues for {{ .ProjectName }}. Enforces a title convention (conventional commit style), a label taxonomy (priority + type), and a structured body template. Every issue gets the same shape, making triage, filtering, and backlog grooming predictable.

---

## Title Convention

Format: `type(scope): concise description`

Same pattern as commits and PR titles. Imperative mood, lowercase, no period, under 72 chars.

### Types

| Type | Use when |
|------|----------|
| `feat` | New feature or capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix or incorrect behavior |
| `refactor` | Code restructure (not a fix, not a feature) |
| `test` | Missing or insufficient test coverage |
| `docs` | Documentation improvement or correction |
| `chore` | Maintenance, dependencies, CI/CD, build system |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |

### Scopes

Use a scope that identifies the affected component or area of the project. Examples:
{{ range .Stacks }}
- `{{ .Name }}` — {{ .Language }}/{{ .Framework }} stack
{{- end }}

Omit scope if it doesn't clarify.

---

## Label Taxonomy

Every issue gets labels from these dimensions. Adapt label names to your repository's conventions.

### Priority (required — exactly 1)

| Label | Color | Use when |
|-------|-------|----------|
| `P0-critical` | #B60205 | Blocks release or causes data loss |
| `P1-high` | #D93F0B | Should fix before next release |
| `P2-medium` | #FBCA04 | Fix when convenient |
| `P3-low` | #0E8A16 | Nice to have, no rush |

### Type (required — exactly 1)

| Label | Color | Use when |
|-------|-------|----------|
| `bug` | #D73A4A | Something is broken |
| `feature` | #0075CA | New capability |
| `tech-debt` | #7057FF | Code quality, robustness, maintainability |
| `docs` | #0E8A16 | Documentation improvement |
| `test-gap` | #E99695 | Missing or insufficient test coverage |

---

## Issue Body Template

Every issue body MUST follow this exact structure:

```markdown
## Context

<!-- One paragraph: where was this found, why does it matter -->

## Current Behavior

<!-- What happens today (or what's missing) -->

## Expected Behavior

<!-- What should happen instead -->

## Files

<!-- Bulleted list of affected files with line numbers if relevant -->
- `path/to/file:42` — description

## Acceptance Criteria

<!-- Checkable items — when is this done? -->
- [ ] criterion 1
- [ ] criterion 2

## Found By

<!-- How this was discovered -->
<!-- Options: PR review (#N), /audit, /doublecheck, manual testing, user report -->
```

---

## Execution Instructions

When this command is invoked, execute the following steps in order.

---

### Step 1 — Parse Arguments

Parse `$ARGUMENTS`:
- If `setup-labels`: jump to the Setup Labels section
- If a title is provided (e.g., `fix(api): handler returns 500`): use it as the title, proceed to Step 3
- If empty: proceed to Step 2 (interactive mode)

---

### Step 2 — Interactive Mode

Prompt the user for:
1. **Type**: one of feat, fix, refactor, test, docs, chore, perf
2. **Scope**: component or area (or omit)
3. **Title description**: concise imperative description
4. Construct the full title: `type(scope): description`

---

### Step 3 — Determine Labels

From the title:
- **Type label**: Map the title type to label (feat->feature, fix->bug, refactor->tech-debt, test->test-gap, docs->docs, chore->tech-debt, perf->tech-debt)
- **Priority**: Ask the user to choose: P0-critical, P1-high, P2-medium, P3-low

---

### Step 4 — Gather Body Content

Ask the user for each section of the body template. For each section, provide a one-line prompt explaining what to write. If the user provides minimal input, expand it into a well-structured paragraph.

For "Found By" — if this was called during a PR review or audit, auto-detect from context (current branch name, recent PR number).

---

### Step 5 — Preview (MANDATORY)

Present the complete issue before creating it:

```
ISSUE PREVIEW

Title: fix(api): handler returns 500 on empty payload
Labels: P2-medium, bug

Body:
## Context
...

## Current Behavior
...

## Expected Behavior
...

## Files
...

## Acceptance Criteria
...

## Found By
...

Create? (yes / edit / cancel)
```

Wait for explicit user approval. If "edit" — accept corrections before continuing.

---

### Step 6 — Create Issue

```bash
gh issue create \
  --title "fix(api): handler returns 500 on empty payload" \
  --label "P2-medium" --label "bug" \
  --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Context
...

## Current Behavior
...

## Expected Behavior
...

## Files
...

## Acceptance Criteria
...

## Found By
...
EOF
)"
```

---

### Step 7 — Confirm

Print the final report:

```
ISSUE CREATED

#N: fix(api): handler returns 500 on empty payload
URL: https://github.com/...
Labels: P2-medium, bug
```

---

## Setup Labels

When `/issue setup-labels` is invoked, create the label taxonomy on GitHub. First delete any default labels that conflict, then create all labels from the taxonomy above.

```bash
# Create priority labels
gh label create "P0-critical" --color "B60205" --description "Blocks release or causes data loss" --force
gh label create "P1-high" --color "D93F0B" --description "Should fix before next release" --force
gh label create "P2-medium" --color "FBCA04" --description "Fix when convenient" --force
gh label create "P3-low" --color "0E8A16" --description "Nice to have, no rush" --force

# Create type labels
gh label create "bug" --color "D73A4A" --description "Something is broken" --force
gh label create "feature" --color "0075CA" --description "New capability" --force
gh label create "tech-debt" --color "7057FF" --description "Code quality, robustness, maintainability" --force
gh label create "docs" --color "0E8A16" --description "Documentation improvement" --force
gh label create "test-gap" --color "E99695" --description "Missing or insufficient test coverage" --force
```

> **Note**: Using `--force` updates existing labels instead of failing. Safe to run multiple times.

---

## What NEVER to Do

- Never create an issue without showing the preview first
- Never skip labels — every issue gets at least 1 priority + 1 type label
- Never create duplicate issues — search first with `gh issue list --search "query"`
- Never write vague titles — "fix bug" or "update docs" are not acceptable
- Never skip the body template sections — all 6 sections are required

---

## Error Conditions

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| `gh` not authenticated | Stop: "Run `gh auth login` first." |
| Label doesn't exist | Run `gh label create` to create it, then retry |
| Duplicate issue found | Show the existing issue, ask user to confirm they want a new one |
| User answers "cancel" at preview | Stop: "No issue created." |
| Title exceeds 72 characters | Shorten before creating |
| Missing scope and can't infer | Ask user to provide one |

---

## Quick Reference

```bash
# Search before creating (avoid duplicates)
gh issue list --search "handler 500" --state open

# Create with labels
gh issue create \
  --title "type(scope): description" \
  --label "P2-medium" --label "bug" \
  --body "body text"

# List open issues by label
gh issue list --label "P3-low" --state open
gh issue list --label "bug" --state open
```
